<!-- ── INTRO ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Garland service across LA</p>
    <h2>The LA Garland authority — 160 years of brand, full lineup coverage, single-call Ali Group family</h2>
    <p>When a Garland goes down in an LA kitchen, the failures our techs at Same Day Appliance Repair run most are the SIT gas-control and burner items that cut across the whole lineup: a SIT thermocouple (the flame safety that shuts the gas if the pilot drops), a gas valve or SIT control, and burner-orifice cleaning on the Master Series and Sentry heavy-duty ranges. On the MCO Master Series convection ovens it's the blower motor, on the GS infrared charbroilers the burner element, and on the ED electric griddles the heating element. We service the full Garland lineup, ranges, MCO convection, GS/GD charbroilers, griddles, Premier/Sunbeam fryers, salamanders, and Hotel Pan Holding, on one Ali Group sibling-call that also covers Frymaster, Manitowoc, Beverage-Air, and Champion, and we stock SIT control components on the truck for same-visit resolution. Typical work runs $200-$340 for a SIT thermocouple, $280-$480 for a gas valve and SIT control, $400-$700 for an MCO convection blower, $320-$560 for a GS infrared burner element, and $700-$1,200 for a control board, most calls $340-$750. Commercial diagnostic is $120, waived with the repair, BHGS #A49573, EPA 608 Universal certified (#1346255700410).</p>
    <p>Across the Garland equipment our techs service in LA, the failures cluster on the gas-control side first. SIT thermocouples lead, the flame-safety device on Garland's gas lineup, an easy and frequent fix. Gas valves and SIT control swaps are next, then burner-orifice cleaning on the heavy-duty ranges. On the MCO convection ovens the blower motor and the heating element lead instead, and on the GS/GD charbroilers the infrared or radiant burner element. ED electric griddle elements and door and oven thermostats fill the mid-band. G-series heavy-duty burner refurbishes are higher on cost, and control boards, rare on Garland but expensive, are the last. Because there's no LA-area Garland authorized service, our techs are the default LA authority, covering the full lineup and stocking SIT parts so most calls close same-visit, and the Ali Group parts chain keeps a used auction Garland serviceable even at 15 years.</p>
    <p class="intro-speakable">Garland Group has manufactured commercial cooking equipment since 1864 — among the oldest continuously-operating commercial range makers in North America. Today the brand is part of Ali Group's foodservice family, following the July 2022 Welbilt Inc. acquisition. Manufacturing continues at the Mississauga, Ontario, Canada plant. The full Garland lineup runs deep — Master Series and Sentry heavy-duty ranges, MCO Master Series convection ovens, GS infrared and GD radiant charbroilers, gas and ED electric griddles, Premier and Sunbeam fryers, Hotel Pan Holding cabinets, salamander broilers. We service every category across all seven Southern California counties on the $120 commercial diagnostic schedule, waived when you approve the repair.</p>
    <p>The Garland manufacturer factory-service network lists training centers in Mayfield Heights, Ohio and Irving, Texas. There is no LA-area Garland authorized service presence. That gap is real, and it shapes who Garland operators in LA actually call when something fails. We have built our Garland practice around being that LA-area authority by default — covering the full product lineup (not just ranges), running the Ali Group sibling-call workflow (Garland + Frymaster + Manitowoc + Beverage-Air + Champion + Lincoln on one service account), and stocking SIT control components on the truck so most service calls resolve same-visit.</p>
    <p>This pillar covers the broader Garland brand. For deep range coverage — M-series electric, G-series gas, US Range sibling, Charbroilers GS-series infrared steakhouse line — we maintain a dedicated detail page at <a href="/brands/garland-range-repair/">/brands/garland-range-repair/</a>. This page extends that with MCO convection ovens, GD radiant charbroilers, griddles, fryers, holding cabinets, and the brand-level operational context.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── OWNERSHIP / HISTORY ── -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Brand ownership and history</p>
    <h2>Garland, Ali Group family member, manufacturing since 1864</h2>
    <p>The current ownership chain: Garland Group → Welbilt Inc. → Ali Group (July 2022). The Welbilt corporate identity ended with that acquisition; references in older SERP content and pre-2022 service-provider sites that describe Garland as "Welbilt Garland" or "Welbilt subsidiary" are out of date by about three years now. The factually accurate framing is Ali Group NA, with the Mississauga, Ontario, Canada manufacturing plant operationally intact under the new parent.</p>
    <p>What that means practically for LA service: Garland sits inside the same Ali Group foodservice portfolio as Frymaster (commercial fryers — McDonald's OEM), Manitowoc Ice (commercial ice machines), Beverage-Air (back-bar refrigeration), Champion and CMA (commercial dishwashers), Convotherm (combi ovens), Lincoln (conveyor pizza ovens), Kolpak / Master-Bilt / Nor-Lake (walk-in coolers), Delfield (commercial refrigeration), and US Range — Garland's direct sibling brand under the same Ali umbrella. Pitco — the other major commercial fryer brand — is NOT part of Ali Group; Pitco is Middleby Corporation family via the Blodgett 1981 acquisition chain. For LA kitchens running multi-Ali equipment configurations (extremely common in hotel banquet operations, steakhouse builds, and Ali-spec dealer packages), one service relationship covers all of it. We carry the parts catalogs and the technician relationships across the full Ali sibling list, plus the Middleby parts pipeline for Pitco coverage.</p>
    <p>The 1864 founding date matters more than it sounds. Garland has continuously operated under various parent structures for 160 years — Welbilt before Ali, manufacturing concerns before Welbilt, original Garland Stove Company before that. Parts continuity is unusually deep as a result. We routinely service Garland equipment from the 1990s and 2000s with the same OEM parts pipeline used for current production. Even legacy Master Series and Sentry units typically have full parts availability.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── PRODUCT LINEUP OVERVIEW ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Full lineup we service</p>
    <h2>The complete Garland commercial cooking lineup</h2>
    <p>Five primary product categories plus specialty configurations. Most LA Garland-equipped kitchens run multiple categories — a steakhouse with Sentry G-series ranges plus GS Charbroilers plus an MCO convection oven, or a hotel banquet kitchen with Master Series ranges plus Premier fryers plus Hotel Pan Holding cabinets. Single service call resolves equipment across the full lineup.</p>

    <div class="product-grid">
      <div class="product-card">
        <h3>Heavy-duty ranges</h3>
        <p><strong>Lines:</strong> Master Series (M electric, US Range gas), Sentry G24 / G36 / G48 / G60, Restaurant Series modular.</p>
        <p><strong>Where in LA:</strong> Steakhouses (Sentry G + Charbroilers), hotel banquet (Master Series + US Range), LAUSD central kitchens (Sentry).</p>
        <p>Deep range coverage on our <a href="/brands/garland-range-repair/">Garland Range combo page</a>.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="product-card">
        <h3>MCO convection ovens</h3>
        <p><strong>Models:</strong> MCO-GS-10S single gas (10 pans), MCO-GS-20 double-stack gas (20 pans), MCO-ES single electric, MCO-ED double electric, Sunfire Series half-size.</p>
        <p><strong>Where in LA:</strong> Mid-segment restaurants, hotel banquet prep, catering production kitchens, school cafeterias.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="product-card">
        <h3>Charbroilers + Salamanders</h3>
        <p><strong>Lines:</strong> GS-series infrared (steakhouse standard, covered in detail on Range combo), GD-series radiant gas (mid-segment grills), Salamander broilers (restaurant finishing and browning stations).</p>
        <p><strong>Where in LA:</strong> Mastro's, Ruth's Chris, Wolfgang's, STK, Capital Grille — premium steakhouse cluster. GD radiant at casual-dining grill concepts. Salamanders at brunch programs and finishing stations.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="product-card">
        <h3>Griddles</h3>
        <p><strong>Models:</strong> G24-G24 / G36-G36 / G48-G48 gas flat-tops (24" / 36" / 48"), ED-30 / ED-48 electric flat-tops, drop-in griddle modules.</p>
        <p><strong>Where in LA:</strong> Diner format operations, hotel breakfast service, brunch-heavy concepts, hospital cafeterias.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="product-card">
        <h3>Fryers</h3>
        <p><strong>Lines:</strong> Premier Series open-pot gas fryers (40-lb, 50-lb capacity), Sunbeam Series tube-fired gas fryers (higher-thermal-recovery for high-volume operations).</p>
        <p><strong>Where in LA:</strong> Operations running a full Garland kitchen package where Premier and Sunbeam came in as part of the Ali-spec dealer build. Second-tier to Pitco/Frymaster in the broader LA install base but consistent presence in Garland-standardized kitchens.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="product-card">
        <h3>Hotel Pan Holding cabinets</h3>
        <p><strong>Use:</strong> Banquet hold and transport, production-then-hold workflows, catering operations. Heated cabinet warmers for hotel pan transport.</p>
        <p><strong>Where in LA:</strong> Hotel banquet kitchens, large catering operations (Patina Restaurant Group, Wolfgang Puck Catering), institutional food service production lines.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── MCO CONVECTION DEEP DIVE ── -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">MCO convection ovens</p>
    <h2>MCO Master Series — the value-positioned convection choice</h2>
    <p>The MCO line is one of Garland's least-marketed but most operationally consequential products in the LA mid-market. When an operator needs convection bake capacity but Rational SCC pricing isn't justified by the menu — bakery production, banquet prep, school-cafeteria sheet-pan throughput — MCO is the answer that lands at 40 to 60 percent of combi-oven price for comparable bake performance.</p>
    <p><strong>MCO-GS-10S.</strong> Single full-size gas convection, 10 standard pans. Workhorse mid-segment unit. We see these at Pasadena restaurants, Beverly Hills bakery production, mid-LA banquet halls. Most common service items: convection blower motor at year 6 to 9 ($400 to $700 installed), door gasket replacement at year 4 to 6 ($150 to $280), SIT gas valve at year 7 to 10.</p>
    <p><strong>MCO-GS-20.</strong> Double-stack gas, 20 pans total. High-volume banquet operations, large hotel kitchen production. DTLA hotel banquet prep frequently runs MCO-GS-20 in pairs for tournament-style service throughput. Service profile mirrors GS-10S with two of each component to monitor.</p>
    <p><strong>MCO-ES and MCO-ED.</strong> Electric variants for kitchens without commercial gas supply or where the operator specifically preferred electric. Heating element replacement runs $300 to $520 — the primary aging failure mode on electric MCO units.</p>
    <p><strong>Sunfire Series.</strong> Half-size convection footprint for cafes, smaller production operations, secondary bake stations. Same gas-vs-electric split as the full MCO line.</p>
    <p><strong>Common diagnostic note:</strong> MCO door gasket failures are the single most common slow-developing problem we see. Operators rarely notice gradual gasket compression loss, but it produces measurable bake-temperature drift across the cavity. When a Pasadena pastry kitchen calls about uneven baking, the first check is door gasket compression — often before the thermostat.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── CHARBROILERS GD + SALAMANDER ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Charbroilers GD and Salamander</p>
    <h2>The non-steakhouse Charbroilers — GD radiant and Salamander broilers</h2>
    <p>The Garland Charbroilers GS-series infrared line gets most of the steakhouse attention (and lives in detail on our Range combo page). But Garland's other broiler products show up frequently in LA mid-market and finishing stations.</p>
    <p><strong>GD-series radiant gas charbroilers.</strong> Lava-rock and radiant configurations for casual-dining grill operations, burger concepts, mid-segment menu programs. The GD architecture uses radiant-plate heat transfer rather than the GS infrared burner system — different service profile, different parts catalog. Most common GD failures: burner orifice clogging ($180 to $300 cleaning), gas valve replacement ($280 to $480), grate replacement at year 6 to 10 ($280 to $480 depending on size). LA install base spans mid-tier steakhouse outposts, burger-focused concepts, and casual-dining grill programs across Mid-City, Hollywood, Long Beach.</p>
    <p><strong>Salamander broilers.</strong> Overhead infrared finishing units for restaurant browning, melting, and au gratin stations. Common at brunch operations, French restaurants, Italian kitchens running cheese-finishing, and steakhouse plating stations. Service items: infrared element burnout (replacement $240 to $420), gas valve, pilot assembly. We see salamanders frequently as paired equipment with Sentry G-series range installations — same operator, same Garland-spec build, both serviceable on one dispatch.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── GRIDDLES ── -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Griddles</p>
    <h2>Garland griddles — diner workhorses and hotel breakfast standards</h2>
    <p>The Garland griddle line is the brand's most diner-restaurant-democratic product. G24/G36/G48 gas flat-tops show up at independent diners, hotel breakfast lines, brunch-heavy concepts, and hospital cafeterias across LA. ED electric variants serve kitchens without commercial gas supply.</p>
    <p><strong>Gas flat-tops (G24-G24, G36-G36, G48-G48).</strong> Standard widths matched to standard counter footprints. Service items: griddle surface re-polishing and re-seasoning at year 4 to 6 ($200 to $340), thermostat calibration drift correction ($200 to $340), gas valve replacement, burner orifice cleaning. Coastal griddles (Santa Monica, Manhattan Beach, Pacific Palisades) corrode faster on burner orifices — we factor that into the service interval recommendation.</p>
    <p><strong>ED-30 and ED-48 electric variants.</strong> Electric flat-tops for kitchens without gas supply or operator preference. Heating element replacement at year 5 to 8 is the primary aging failure ($300 to $520 installed). Element failure typically starts with one zone running cold while others stay hot — diagnostic confirms element open-circuit before swap.</p>
    <p><strong>Drop-in griddle modules.</strong> Garland also produces drop-in griddle modules for modular cooking suites (paired with the heavy-duty range line). Same service architecture as freestanding units but installed within Master Series or US Range builds. We service both configurations.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── FRYERS + HOLDING ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Fryers and Holding cabinets</p>
    <h2>Premier, Sunbeam, and Hotel Pan Holding — the rest of the Garland kitchen</h2>
    <p>Garland fryers don't dominate the LA commercial fryer market — that's Pitco and Frymaster territory. Frymaster is Garland's Ali Group fryer sibling (both came over with the July 2022 Welbilt acquisition); Pitco is a cross-corporate competitor under Middleby Corporation. But where an operator standardized on a full Garland kitchen package, Premier and Sunbeam fryers come as part of the build. We service them as readily as the rest of the Garland line.</p>
    <p><strong>Premier Series open-pot gas fryers.</strong> 40-lb and 50-lb capacity configurations. Open-pot design simplifies cleaning and oil management compared to tube-fired variants. Service items: high-limit thermostat ($240 to $380), pilot assembly + thermocouple ($200 to $340), gas valve ($280 to $480). Common failure: pilot thermocouple and safety valve failing as a pair (same age, same heat-cycle exposure). We replace both together when one fails to avoid the inevitable return visit.</p>
    <p><strong>Sunbeam Series tube-fired gas fryers.</strong> Higher thermal-recovery output for high-volume operations. Tube-burner architecture differs from Premier open-pot — different service approach but same SIT-control safety system. Tube-burner inspection at year 5 to 7 catches early carbon buildup that affects recovery time.</p>
    <p><strong>Hotel Pan Holding cabinets.</strong> Heated cabinets for banquet hold and catering transport. Service items are simple — heating element replacement, thermostat calibration, door gasket replacement. We see Hotel Pan Holding heavily at LA catering operations (Patina, Wolfgang Puck Catering) and hotel banquet production-to-service workflows. Failure modes are forgiving — even a degraded cabinet stays operationally serviceable while the part is ordered.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── SIT CONTROL ASSEMBLY ── -->
<section class="section section-alt">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">SIT control assembly</p>
    <h2>SIT — Garland's gas safety system across the entire product line</h2>
    <p>If you call us about a Garland that won't light, has intermittent burner cutout, or shows no-pilot symptoms, the diagnostic almost always lands on SIT. SIT (Sicurezza Italiana Tecnologica) is the Italian-engineered gas safety control assembly Garland standardized on across most of its gas-fired equipment — ranges, convection ovens, fryers, charbroilers. Thermocouple, safety valve, pilot assembly, and the integrated module that ties them together.</p>
    <p>The SIT design is reliable, but it has wear-out failure modes. Thermocouples drift open-circuit at year 5 to 8 in heavy duty. Safety valves degrade with thermal cycling. Pilot assemblies foul with combustion residue over time. Garland's own service training materials (the Mayfield Heights training center curriculum specifically) dedicate substantial coverage to SIT removal and replacement — confirming what we see in the field: SIT components are the most common Garland service intervention across categories.</p>
    <p>What we keep on the van for same-visit resolution: SIT thermocouples in the most common configurations ($45 to $95 part cost), SIT safety valves ($180 to $280), pilot assemblies. Most Garland gas equipment SIT calls resolve in 30 to 60 minutes once we confirm the diagnosis, including testing the safety cycle before signing off. The full repair runs $200 to $480 parts-and-labor depending on which SIT component failed.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── LA SERVICE MAP ── -->
<section class="section">
