<!-- ── USED GARLAND ── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="container narrow">
    <p class="eyebrow-sub">Used Garland market</p>
    <h2>Restaurant liquidation pipeline — used Garland in LA</h2>
    <p>LA's restaurant turnover rate produces a steady stream of used Garland equipment hitting auction and liquidation channels. Restaurant Equipment World, IRS auctions, RestaurantEquipment.com listings, plus the direct-dealer-liquidation channel — all of these put used Garland in front of LA operators at meaningful discount to new pricing.</p>
    <p><strong>Typical LA used-Garland economics:</strong></p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li>Used Sentry G-series ranges run $1,200 to $3,500 (versus $8,000 to $15,000 new).</li>
      <li>Used MCO-GS-10S convection runs $1,800 to $4,500 (versus $7,000 to $14,000 new).</li>
      <li>Used Premier and Sunbeam fryers run $600 to $1,800 (versus $3,000 to $6,000 new).</li>
      <li>Used Hotel Pan Holding cabinets run $400 to $1,200 (versus $1,800 to $3,500 new).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We don't sell Garland equipment. We service it regardless of how you acquired it. The honest framing: if you bought used at auction and you need a tech who knows the SIT control assembly architecture, the Master Series burner setup, and the MCO blower-motor replacement procedure, we are that tech. We won't shame you for not buying new.</p>
    <p>The serviceability math typically works out. Garland's parts continuity is unusually deep for a 160-year-old brand — most components on 10 to 15-year-old units are still in production or have direct-replacement OEM equivalents. A $850 repair on a $2,500 used Sentry that retails $9,000 new is a clear operational win. We give the honest assessment after the $120 diagnostic and we will tell you straight if a unit is past economic repair.</p>
  </div>
</section>

